<section id="asks" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">1</span>What this page asks</h2>
<p>Thirteen of your entries in the record of decisions have new reasons, written after the foundations review. Nothing enters the record until you say yes. You can answer "yes", or name an entry to change by its section number, such as 4.4.</p>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>The thirteen entries and your approval: <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/60aaeb6bb0f74dcb6018b0b5ceb268b0834da50e/explorations/reviews/foundations/rewrite-results.md#what-was-rewritten">the rewrite's results, "What was rewritten"</a>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="test" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">2</span>The test, in plain words</h2>
<p>The test checks that the new texts lead the coordinator to the right choice. The coordinator is the session that plans the work and runs the agents.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Four cases.</strong> Each is a case that a planner or a worker could bring to the coordinator. A planner is the agent that drafts a batch of work; a worker builds one piece of it. The cases were written before the new texts existed, so that the old texts lead the wrong way.</li>
<li><strong>Three readers.</strong> Two agents each acted as the coordinator, on a copy of the repository without the review. One had the old texts, the other the new ones. A third agent graded both answers without knowing which reader had which texts. Each case scores 3 points: 2 for the decision, 1 for the sources cited.</li>
<li><strong>The result.</strong> The new texts: 12 of 12. The old texts: 10 of 12. Both readers cited their sources in full. The old texts lost one point of decision in each of two cases.</li>
<li><strong>Where the old texts misled: the order traits' self type.</strong> The order traits are the library's traits for comparing values. Their self type stands for the type that implements the trait. A planner proposed to copy the compiler's prelude's spelling of it, <code>comprises T</code>, into the one library, as "the team's later word". The compiler's prelude is the compiler's own small library. The one library is the library of walk, the interpreter; both paths are to use it. The reader with the old texts dropped the copy, which was right, but on a weaker ground: the library's own practice. And it kept the copy open, to be tested by a probe and then put to you as a question. A probe is a small experiment. The reader had the dates: Naden's text of 31 August 2012 is later than Steele's prelude edits of 5 and 6 June 2012. But no old text made the date decide. The new later-word and one-library entries do.</li>
<li><strong>Where the old texts misled: <code>System</code> on the compiled path.</strong> <code>System</code> is the component, the part of the library, that gives a program its arguments and its environment. A planner proposed to keep the compiler's own <code>System</code>, <code>CompilerSystem</code>, on the compiled path after the switch-over. The switch-over is the point where the compiled path moves onto the one library and the compiler's prelude is deleted. The reader with the old texts refused, but offered a <code>CompilerSystem</code> bridge as one option of a question to you. The old one-library entry gives no reason for one library. The new one gives yours: the switch-over exists so that walk's library is not ported into the compiler's.</li>
<li><strong>The other two cases.</strong> A worker offered to make a defect of the code generator a rule of the language. Another offered to fix a checker defect by copying a range trait from the compiler's prelude into the one library. In both cases, both readers chose right.</li>
</ol>
