<section id="answers" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">3</span>The answers these texts assume</h2>
<p>Four of your answers are still open. The texts are written under the default of each. If you answer another way, the texts change as each point says.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Phase 3 closes with climb batch 22c, over fourteen components.</strong> Phase 3 is the work that makes the type checker accept the library's core components without error. A component is one part of the library, such as <code>List</code>. A climb batch is one run of the workflow in which agents build a set of rungs; a rung is one piece of work. The distance stage is the tool that counts the checker's errors over the library's twelve core components. The fourteen are those twelve, plus the two that <code>List</code> imports. Default: yes. If no: the one-library and phases entries count the twelve, not the fourteen, and the phases entry counts 40 other components, not 38.</li>
<li><strong>The 38 other components block a later step, not the switch-over.</strong> The later step is the one at which walk, the interpreter, runs the checker on its programs, after the switch-over. Each component is cleared before that step, or before the first program that needs it. The judgement wrote 40, but two of the 40 are <code>List</code>'s imports, which phase 3 now counts. Default: yes. If no: the one-library and phases entries say that the switch-over also needs those components to pass the checker and the code generator, and the entry on walk and the checker says they are cleared by then.</li>
<li><strong>Three one-word answers.</strong> Defaults: drop "functions" from what the bound's entry says <code>Any</code> holds; strike the sentence "The implementers' later word also outweighs unfinished text." from the later-word entry; move the flat-numbers entry's two "Why" sentences to the record's history, the file that keeps each entry's earlier texts. If you keep any of these, that entry keeps its old words: the bound's entry says "tuples, functions and <code>()</code>"; the later-word entry keeps the sentence; the flat-numbers entry keeps its two sentences and drops the one that calls the prelude's flat numbers "not a ground".</li>
<li><strong>Your lean toward the class loader, recorded as a lean to confirm.</strong> On 20 September you leaned toward the class loader as the way to give an <code>RR64</code> array, made through the library's generic array traits, a Java <code>double[]</code> store. The class loader is the part of the compiled path that makes Java classes while a program runs. Default: record it. If no: the array-questions entry drops its new sentence, and the array-store entry drops its last clause, which points to the lean.</li>
</ol>
