<section id="e1" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">4.1</span>The team's later word wins</h2>
<ol>
<li>New: three layers, defined here once for the record. The language: the specification, the team's papers on types, and the team's library. The team's build on the JVM: the checker, the code generator, the class loader, the run time, the compiler's prelude and walk. Java interoperability: how Fortress types and natives meet Java's. The frame is yours, of 26 October.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: a ground in one layer may confirm a question in another, but doesn't decide it. A JVM or checker limit never overrides the language's texts. Where they give no answer, you decide by the goal.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: "later" is a date. The language's texts are ranked by year, from the Working Draft, the specification's text as the team left it in 2010, to the 2019 paper. The prelude's number types of 2009 and 2010 leave the list of the later word: that code is earlier than the 2011 paper. The team's code counts as their word only where they wrote the rule on purpose; a stub or a workaround doesn't.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: where two later sources conflict, you decide by the goal, and the record says so. The two meanings of "specialisation" get two names: a specialised overload, in the language, and stamping, in the build.</li>
<li>Your words stay. The title says "the team's later word", as you put it. Your reason, now headed "Why", says the early text was written "early and in a vacuum", and the revival "does not second-guess them". The sentence "The implementers' later word also outweighs unfinished text." is struck, under your open answer; it had no source.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The new text</h3>
<div class="entry-text"><p>P-26 <strong>Where the team's later word on the language conflicts with the specification's early text, the later word wins; each ground is weighed in its own layer, and "later" is a date.</strong> The team's word has three layers. The language: the Working Draft of February 2011, whose text the team last edited in 2010-12, and <code>Specification/</code>, the revival's revision of it (P-7); the 2012 Types chapter; the type group's papers; and the team's library, the interpreter's, as their statement of the language's library. A team text of the language that argues from Java, as the 2011 paper does, still counts in the language. The team's implementation on the JVM: the compiled checker, the code generator, the class loader, the run time, the compiler's prelude and walk. Java interoperability: how Fortress types and natives meet Java's. A ground in one layer may confirm, but does not by itself decide, a question in another, and a JVM or checker limit never overrides what the language's texts say. Where those texts give no answer, the curator decides by the goal (P-8), and a ground in the implementation may weigh there (P-77). In the language, later texts that agree with each other rank above earlier ones, by year: the Working Draft (text of 2010), the type group's paper (2011, <code>Papers/Types/</code>), the Types chapter and Karl Naden's write-up "The Return Type Rule and Generics" (both 2012, the write-up at <code>Papers/Types/journal/justificationOfRTR.tex</code>), and the POPL 2019 paper, the last (F-166). Where the specification conflicts with them, it is revised to follow them (P-95 records each change with its reason). The team's code in the implementation counts as their word on the language only where they wrote the rule on purpose: in a text, a commit message, a code comment, or a checker rule enforced as the language's. A stub by the team's own words, such as a line they called "bogus" or a "temporary hack", does not count, and neither does a workaround of a JVM or checker gap. Before you call a commit later, date it against the texts: a 2009 change to the compiler's prelude is earlier than the 2011 paper. Where two later sources conflict, this rule gives no order: the curator decides by the goal (P-8), and the record says that the goal decided it. Two things are called specialisation. A specialised overload is a version of a generic function for one instantiation, such as <code>tail(x: List[ZZ])</code> beside <code>tail[X](x: List[X])</code>, which dispatch chooses at run time; it belongs to the language. Stamping is the class loader's making of one class for each instantiation; it belongs to the implementation. Why: the early text was a wish, written early and in a vacuum; the type group learned what is feasible by building it, with performance as the goal and specialised overloads a must; the revival knows less than they did and does not second-guess them. That reason is evidence about the implementation. The ground in the language: the Working Draft contradicts itself, since its types chapter makes the number types mutually exclusive and its library chapters make ℤ a subtype of ℚ, and the later texts agree with each other on the exclusion rule, the overloading rules and the default bound.</p></div>
