nownNames.java:113-116</code>); its designs are evidence, not authority. A prelude design that differs from the library's binds the one library in two cases only: the team's later word on the language backs it, as the exclusion rule backs the flat number types (P-43) and the team's later export check backs the declaration of abstract members (P-158); or the language's text is silent or ambiguous and the team's compiled checker enforces the design as a rule. A prelude line counts as the team's later word only if it is later than the library's text on the same shape, by P-26's dating, and no later text of the language contradicts it. A stub does not bind, and neither does a workaround of a JVM or checker gap, such as fixed arities in place of varargs. This decision does not stop work on the code generator, which is compiler work under either library. The decision is not put to the curator again. How the checker comes to accept the library, its first step and the order of the work, is the plan that the curator approved (P-33).</p></div>
